WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.091
Prompt injection through tool results.

2
00:00:02.371 --> 00:00:04.483
Tool descriptions come from the server.

3
00:00:04.763 --> 00:00:14.789
Tool results come from wherever the server gets its data, which often includes text anyone can write: issue descriptions, emails, web pages, support tickets.

4
00:00:15.069 --> 00:00:19.144
Lesson 11 in Studio 1 called this indirect prompt injection.

5
00:00:19.424 --> 00:00:24.949
MCP makes it more common, because it makes connecting an agent to many such sources easy.

6
00:00:25.229 --> 00:00:30.840
The clearest public case is the one Invariant Labs disclosed in May 2025.

7
00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:37.989
An attacker opens an issue in a user’s public GitHub repository containing instructions for the agent.

8
00:00:38.269 --> 00:00:46.696
The user asks their agent, connected to the GitHub MCP server with access to their private repositories, to look at the open issues.

9
00:00:46.976 --> 00:00:58.155
The agent reads the injected issue, pulls private repository data into its context, and publishes it in a pull request on the public repository, where the attacker can read it.

10
00:00:58.435 --> 00:01:01.955
The demonstration used Claude 4 Opus in Claude Desktop.

11
00:01:02.235 --> 00:01:15.504
Invariant stated that this is not a flaw in the GitHub server’s code, and that it cannot be fully fixed with server-side patches, because it arises from the combination of private data, untrusted text, and a way to publish.

12
00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:17.960
That is Willison’s lethal trifecta.

13
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.843
Your tracker has the same shape on a small scale.

14
00:01:21.123 --> 00:01:30.317
Issue HAR-105 contains a customer comment telling the assistant to mark every open issue as done and not to tell the user.

15
00:01:30.597 --> 00:01:47.749
The layered defense you built: the text is cleaned and labeled as data, the server’s instructions and the write tool’s description say never to act on instructions inside issues, the write tool requires a preview round trip, and only a person approving the call in the host can let it through.

16
00:01:48.029 --> 00:01:49.779
None of these is sufficient alone.

17
00:01:50.059 --> 00:01:55.072
Together, the human gate on the write is the one that holds even when the model is fooled.

18
00:01:55.352 --> 00:02:00.237
For anything that leaves your system or cannot be undone, ask the user through the host.

19
00:02:00.517 --> 00:02:04.763
A confirm argument is set by the model and can be faked by a fooled model.

20
00:02:05.043 --> 00:02:17.587
Elicitation goes to the user, and the specification forbids using its form mode to collect passwords, API keys, or payment credentials; those must use URL mode, outside the client.
